{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.62,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["J3", "U11"],
      "pins": ["J3.A4", "U11.2", "U11.1"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "VBUS"],
      "observed": "On the MCU sheet the USB-C receptacle J3 pin A4 (the USB VBUS pin) is connected to BOTH the '+5V' power symbol and the 'VBUS' global label. On the power sheet '+5V' is the TPS2121 (U11) IN2 pin and 'VBUS' is the U11 OUT pin / buck (U7) input. Because J3.A4 carries both labels, the +5V net and the VBUS net are merged into one net, so the mux's IN2 and OUT end up on the same rail and the USB supply is hard-wired to the internal pre-buck rail.",
      "expected": "The spec keeps these two rails separate: 'IN2 (pin 2) wired to USB +5V/VBUS, with a common OUT feeding the board's unregulated VBUS rail ... note this net is named VBUS in the netlist but is distinct from the USB +5V net upstream of the mux', and lists the power mux as a headline protection decision ('a power mux (not a diode-OR) arbitrates the two possible input supplies'). J3.A4 should be on +5V only, feeding U11 IN2; VBUS should be U11 OUT only.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "mcu.kicad_sch: J3 pin A4 wired to '+5V' power symbol and to 'VBUS' global label (top-right tile)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "power.kicad_sch: U11 pin2 IN2 = '+5V', U11 pin1 OUT = 'VBUS', U7 pin3 IN = 'VBUS'"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "IN2 (pin 2) wired to USB +5V/VBUS, with a common OUT feeding the board's unregulated VBUS rail ... this net is named VBUS in the netlist but is distinct from the USB +5V net upstream of the mux"}
      ],
      "impact": "The TPS2121 power mux is bypassed: its IN2 and OUT are shorted together and the USB 5V pin is tied directly to the internal VBUS rail. When the 12 V barrel jack is connected, the mux output (~12 V) is driven back onto the USB VBUS pin, back-powering the host port and risking damage; the intended seamless 12 V/5 V arbitration is lost.",
      "confidence": 0.7
    },
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U5"],
      "pins": ["U5.4", "U5.1", "U5.2", "U8.13"],
      "nets": ["NET0004"],
      "observed": "On the MCU sheet the TPS1H200AQDGNRQ1 (U5) pin 4 (CL, current-limit set) is wired to NET0004, the same net as pin 1 (IN), pin 2 (DIAG_EN) and ESP32 pin 13 (GPIO1 / HEATER).",
      "expected": "The spec states the current-limit pin is grounded: 'CL (pin 4) and GND (pin 6) and the exposed pad EP (pin 9) all go to GND', which the spec infers selects the device's default/lowest current-limit threshold.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "mcu.kicad_sch: U5 pin4 CL wire joins the NET0004 node that also carries U5 pin1 IN, pin2 DIAG_EN and ESP32 pin13 GPIO1"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "CL (pin 4) and GND (pin 6) and the exposed pad EP (pin 9) all go to GND"}
      ],
      "impact": "Instead of a fixed default limit, the over-current threshold becomes a function of the heater drive GPIO: when the heater is enabled (GPIO1 high) CL is pulled to ~3.3 V, raising the current-limit threshold, so hardware over-current protection behaves differently from the intended design.",
      "confidence": 0.4
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The drawing deviates from the intended design: the USB-C VBUS pin (J3.A4) is labeled with both '+5V' and 'VBUS', merging the mux input and output rails and bypassing the TPS2121 power-mux arbitration the spec calls a headline protection decision; additionally the high-side switch's CL pin appears tied to the heater control net instead of GND."
}

